<section class="sub" id="nmhp-government">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">GOVERNMENT-PUBLISHED PLANS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Three levels of government, three official housing plans</h2>
<p class="section-note">Each adopted or formally released by the government body named — not independent commentary on housing policy. These are what the General Plan and Land Development Code updates can build on rather than invent from scratch.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">

<article class="card case-study" id="nmhp-state">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>New Mexico Housing Strategy: A Call to Action — 2026 Update</h3>
<span class="cs-tag gov">State of New Mexico · MFA</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Housing New Mexico (the state's Mortgage Finance Authority) and Root Policy Research's statewide roadmap, first published in 2022 and refreshed in 2026 with more than 20 focus groups and a 1,300-response resident survey. It organizes the state's housing work into four action areas: produce housing across the income continuum, preserve and redevelop existing affordable stock, build homeownership opportunity (especially for low/moderate-income and racially diverse households), and create housing stability for people experiencing or at risk of homelessness.</p>
<span class="field-label">What's been accomplished</span>
<p>Annually between 2022 and 2025, state housing funding supported roughly 1,000 new affordable units, rehabilitated or weatherized 800 homes, helped 2,400 households reach homeownership, kept 3,200 households out of homelessness and 5,700 out of eviction, and assisted 3,700 homeless or at-risk individuals with services. Housing New Mexico has financed 20,822 income-restricted units statewide to date.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">The strategy's own white paper on housing production cites Oregon's statewide middle-housing legalization and Minneapolis 2040 as evidence for what upzoning can do — both are already profiled in depth on this library's <a href="/cities/portland/">Portland</a> and <a href="/cities/minneapolis/">Minneapolis</a> pages and in <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/">Effective General Plan Reforms</a>; this page doesn't repeat that material. The MFA's May 2026 board presentation on this update (Item 3) is a condensed version of the same findings, not a separate source.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://housingnm.org/uploads/documents/New_Mexico_Housing_Strategy_2026_Update.pdf">Housing New Mexico — 2026 Housing Strategy Update (PDF) ↗</a>
<a href="https://housingnm.org/the-new-mexico-housing-strategy">Housing New Mexico — strategy overview ↗</a></div>
</article>

<article class="card case-study" id="nmhp-city">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>Five-Year Affordable Housing Strategic Plan</h3>
<span class="cs-tag gov">City of Santa Fe</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>The City's own Office of Affordable Housing plan, prepared by Root Policy Research. It puts a hard number on the zoning question this whole update turns on: 76% of the city's residential land — developed and undeveloped — is zoned at 5 dwelling units per acre or less, essentially unchanged from the 78% figure in the city's 2017 affordable housing plan. The draft plan organizes its recommendations around three principles: increasing housing production, increasing stabilization against displacement, and expanding services for residents with the highest housing-instability risk.</p>
<span class="field-label">Key recommendations</span>
<p><b>Land Donation</b> — prioritize city-owned and underused corridor land with existing infrastructure, naming the Siler Road corridor and the Midtown LINC district at St. Michael's Drive. <b>Land Use Reform</b> — as the city pursues its code rewrite, remove barriers to production, update outdated parking and height standards, and create multi-use zoning with variable densities. <b>Incremental Infill</b> — support ADU and casita construction through financing connections and toolkits for individual homeowners. <b>Regional Housing Policy</b> — coordinate across city and county lines rather than treating Santa Fe's housing market as bounded by the city limits.</p>
<span class="field-label">What residents said</span>
<p>Survey and focus-group participants named more affordable rental housing, more affordable homeownership, and a response to homelessness as the top three needs. Specific ideas raised included shared-living arrangements for single and elderly residents, smaller apartments in the $600–800/month range, replicating models like Siler Yard and Santa Fe Suites, and incentives for landlords to accept housing vouchers.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://santafenm.gov/OAH_Five_Year_Strategy_Plan_-_COSF.pdf">City of Santa Fe — Five-Year Affordable Housing Strategic Plan (PDF) ↗</a>
<a href="https://santafenm.gov/news/city-releases-five-year-strategic-housing-plan-draft">City of Santa Fe — plan release announcement ↗</a></div>
</article>

<article class="card case-study" id="nmhp-county">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>2023 Santa Fe County Affordable Housing Plan</h3>
<span class="cs-tag gov">Santa Fe County</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>Santa Fe County's own plan, approved with a New Mexico Mortgage Finance Authority letter. It's the county, not the city — but its density-cost modeling is the most concrete local evidence in this batch for why density and affordability are linked here specifically, not just in other markets. For-purchase housing becomes reasonably affordable to average Santa Fe County residents at roughly 10 dwelling units per acre; building at that density instead of 1 unit per acre cuts development costs by about 49%. Rental housing reaches similar affordability around 14–20 units per acre. Outside the Community College District, the County's own code (SLDC) typically caps growth-area density at 1–2.5 units per acre.</p>
<span class="field-label">Key recommendations</span>
<p>Two linked strategies: first, amend the SLDC to formally define "Affordable Housing Development" and attach incentives — fee waivers, flexibility, alternative compliance — to projects that meet the definition; second, broader structural SLDC changes (adjusting open-space dedication requirements and use-mix phasing rules that add cost without adding affordability) to grow the supply of naturally occurring affordable housing, not only subsidized units.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">A reminder that Santa Fe's housing market functions regionally — a city-only General Plan and Land Development Code update doesn't reach county-adjacent growth, where a large share of the metro's lowest-density, most car-dependent development already happens.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://go.boarddocs.com/nm/sfc/Board.nsf/files/CTXJCH4C851F/$file/Exhibit%20B%20-%202023%20Santa%20Fe%20County%20Affordable%20Housing%20Plan%20with%20NMMFA%20Approval%20Letter.pdf">Santa Fe County — 2023 Affordable Housing Plan with NMMFA approval letter (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>

</div>
