<section class="sub" id="nmhp-research">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">INDEPENDENT RESEARCH</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Not a government document — policy research from outside City Hall</h2>
<p class="section-note">Published by a university-affiliated policy center, not any government body. Its recommendations are the authors' own analysis, not an official city, county, or state position — useful context, not a plan this update is bound to.</p>
</div>
<div class="case-study-list">

<article class="card case-study" id="nmhp-permitting">
<div class="card-top">
<h3>How to Streamline Housing Permitting in New Mexico</h3>
<span class="cs-tag research">Independent research · Mercatus Center</span>
</div>
<span class="field-label">Description</span>
<p>An August 2026 policy brief from the Mercatus Center at George Mason University (Gilligan, Toledo, Lindquist, and Furth, drawing on roughly 20 interviews with developers, planners, and officials) focused specifically on the permitting process, not the underlying zoning. New Mexico's housing gap is put at 58,000 homes or more; rents rose 48% and home values 85% from 2015 to 2025. New Mexico is named as the only state in the Southwest that hasn't passed state-level housing-permitting reform.</p>
<span class="field-label">Recommended local practices</span>
<p>Elevate public input to the plan and code level — through regular Comprehensive Plan and code updates — so individual permit decisions can be administrative rather than re-litigated case by case. Make administrative, by-right approval the default for projects that meet adopted standards, reserving discretionary hearings for large or unusual developments. Where a development is required to fund off-site infrastructure, keep that requirement within the U.S. Supreme Court's "rough proportionality" and "rational nexus" tests.</p>
<span class="field-label">Recommended state legislation</span>
<p>Capacity-building funds for jurisdictions that voluntarily adopt permitting best practices. A permanent state Division of Housing — New Mexico currently has no agency charged with tracking housing data, supporting local governments, and driving permitting reform, unlike Colorado or Washington.</p>
<span class="field-label">Considerations</span>
<p class="muted">The brief cites in-state precedent directly relevant to Santa Fe's own LDC update: Las Cruces paired a 2020 comprehensive plan update with a 2025 zoning rewrite ("Realize Las Cruces") that legalized multiunit housing and ADUs citywide and reduced lot-size, setback, and parking minimums; Farmington is cited as a model for administrative approval. These map onto the By-Right/Ministerial Approval and Expedited Review Deadline concepts already covered in <a href="/research/ldc-concepts/">Land Development Code Concepts</a> — this brief adds New Mexico-specific legal and legislative context those entries don't. As with any outside research, treat its recommendations as one perspective to weigh, not an adopted position.</p>
<span class="field-label">Sources</span>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.mercatus.org/research/policy-briefs/how-streamline-housing-permitting-new-mexico">Mercatus Center — full policy brief ↗</a>
